:answer/value "images/40/40490df9d6ec4cc44759b0265e9355dfe6edba66028e61d6e5c36c0d839388d9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f3d40c-2faf-5526-b31d-531299ba0057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c5/c52f568868328061237d43e4650180321ec4a40e886a62f4a678f0276afe821b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395375} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dcbea8f-b000-5d95-8095-fceedf72d8ad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b5276417-1d46-5a8a-a5e9-31a83ac43586" :question/math-academy-id "q-98004" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot $y=\\frac{1}{2(x+1)},$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n![](images/a3/a32495fcca947d01cf8d3218fc5cfc0cce083a34e6fb6b629d92ba9e47039a6e.png)\n\n- Translate it by $1$ unit to the left to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{x+1}.$\n\n![](images/04/049f9c306c007026bc72e5f00d925cf22d80d94049aa82e3c81d7fc7c289249f.png)\n\n- Then, stretch the result by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2(x+1)}.$\n\n![](images/c5/c52f568868328061237d43e4650180321ec4a40e886a62f4a678f0276afe821b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4e178c7-bdb4-5139-a20c-3e17de51ee11" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Distance Between Two Points With Variable Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81b1c11b-b4b1-536c-8889-3a3eb09882dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}\\,w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395425} {:answer/id #uuid "c96ad5b8-0210-5052-9081-f410de18a5e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395426} {:answer/id #uuid "398a8fd3-2eed-529b-9541-d6be85bc82d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395427} {:answer/id #uuid "04663b1a-f275-5578-853a-d2e34a0425ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\,w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395428} {:answer/id #uuid "16d6cb4d-d2f4-501f-8ae8-ed5949dd56c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395429}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16d6cb4d-d2f4-501f-8ae8-ed5949dd56c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}\\,w" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395429} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea09eb90-beeb-5d00-94ad-88249fe8aeca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395424}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb9ffaa1-a202-507f-aa7e-4d95a973e5e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-98142" :question/problem "Calculate the distance between the points $(3w,2w,7w)$ and $(-w,6w,3w),$ given that $w$ is positive." :question/worked-solution "The distance $d$ between two points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1},{z}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2},{z}_{2})$ is given by\n\n$$\nd=\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}{)}^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1}{)}^{2}+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1}{)}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have ${{(3w,2w,7w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{, }{y}_{1}\\text{, }{z}_{1}\\text{)}}$ and ${{(-w,6w,3w)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{, }{y}_{2}\\text{, }{z}_{2}\\text{)}}.$ Applying the distance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\sqrt{(-w-3w{)}^{2}+(6w-2w{)}^{2}+(3w-7w{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4w{)}^{2}+(4w{)}^{2}+(-4w{)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{w}^{2}+16{w}^{2}+16{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{48}\\,\\sqrt{{w}^{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}\\,|w|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $w$ is positive, we have $|w|=w.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nd=4\\sqrt{3}\\,w.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362320 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41db8918-3463-50ca-af08-d975bde2dbca" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Rational Equations Using the Lowest Common Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "118da805-8d4d-5712-a36c-cd77c1c5bcc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360902} {:answer/id #uuid "221ed9dd-6363-58f5-a13f-28ea35802178" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360987} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8d4542-dea0-5420-b58c-12dbab7429c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361030} {:answer/id #uuid "3432d0b7-a400-5e44-af3e-694272f1758a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361067} {:answer/id #uuid "7608caaa-0a67-5ff3-8a2a-4143e6b8492c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361359}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b8d4542-dea0-5420-b58c-12dbab7429c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186361030} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca620ff8-9a8a-5856-bc6f-86f00cb0ed6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186362168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2197596-2744-5571-ba37-b2f03d9b6d9e" :question/math-academy-id "q-98716" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{4}{x(x+2)}.$" :question/worked-solution "We make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,\\,x+2,\\,x(x+2))=x(x+2).\n$$\n\n- The values $x=0$ and $x=-2$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-\\frac{1}{x+2} & =\\frac{4}{x(x+2)} \\\\ x(x+2)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{x-1}-\\frac{1}{x+2}) & =x(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{4}{x(x+2)} \\\\ \\frac{2x(x+2)}{x}-\\frac{x(x+2)}{x+2} & =\\frac{4x(x+2)}{x(x+2)} \\\\ \\frac{2\\cancel{x}\\cdot (x+2)}{\\cancel{x}}-\\frac{x\\cdot \\cancel{(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x+2}} & =\\frac{4\\cdot \\cancel{x(x+2)}}{\\cancel{x(x+2)}} \\\\ 2(x+2)-x & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $x,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+2)-x & =4 \\\\ 2x+4-x & =4 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $x=0$ is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea2f1348-55bf-5313-8d5e-a5a097231e97" :knowledge-point/title "Graphing a Stretched Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a4dc8da-0af6-521a-94e2-29efe502a4ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e2/e2a1274fc65341a659bb76923bb69677cc33bad1b78dfdf64ba97e0c8a00fb9c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068423} {:answer/id #uuid "b6cfcb00-3352-501b-8c96-ce26e77b5a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db